          "question": "Why is it important that datasets are curated and what are some of the risks of using uncurated datasets? (5 Marks)",
          "modelAnswer": "Why curate: curation is the organisation, selection and integration of data from various sources. Language models learn whatever is in their data, so curation is how you control what they learn: removing social biases, anonymising to preserve privacy, removing exploitable patterns and balancing the data so groups and languages are fairly represented.\n\nRisks of uncurated (e.g. raw web) data:\n• Hegemonic view: dominant voices on the internet are over-represented, so models encode and amplify their views and biases, and under-represented groups and languages are served worse.\n• Toxic or harmful content and stereotypes are reproduced.\n• Privacy leaks: personal information is memorised and can be regurgitated.\n• Generated content feeds back into training data (dead-internet theory), leading to bias propagation and model collapse.\n• Duplication, misinformation, low quality, and exploitable patterns (adversarial triggers).\n\n(Curation has costs too: the curator can be biased, data is thrown away, and there is no single best approach.)",
