          "id": "nlp-new-060",
          "lecture": 5,
          "concept": "RNN vs CNN language models",
          "type": "written",
          "marks": 3,
          "question": "Explain why recurrent networks were preferred over CNNs for generating text with a language model. (3 Marks)",
          "modelAnswer": "Generation is autoregressive: after every new token the model must predict again with a longer context.\nAn RNN keeps a hidden state summarising everything so far, so each new token needs only one recurrent step: no recomputation.\nA CNN (with global pooling) has no running memory: to predict the next token it must rerun the convolutions and pooling over the whole growing sequence, redoing work every step. The lecturer's wider point: in NLP, faster training/generation with a small memory footprint wins because it lets you use more data.",
