          "lecture": 6,
          "concept": "The bottleneck",
          "type": "written",
          "marks": 4,
          "question": "Explain the 'fixed source representation' problem of a seq2seq model without attention, from the point of view of both the encoder and the decoder, and how attention solves it. (4 Marks)",
          "modelAnswer": "Encoder: it must compress the whole source sentence into one fixed-size vector. That gets harder (noisier) as sentences get longer, and early tokens fade through the recurrence.\nDecoder: it needs different, specific information at different steps (the word for 'cat' now, the word for 'mat' later), but only has one summary vector, set once at the start.\nAttention: at every decoder step, score all encoder states against the current decoder state, softmax the scores into weights, and take the weighted sum as a source context vector. The decoder can then retrieve the relevant source details at each step instead of relying on the single bottleneck vector.",
