          "marks": 4,
          "question": "Describe beam search for decoding, and give one advantage and one disadvantage compared with greedy decoding. (4 Marks)",
          "modelAnswer": "Beam search keeps the k (beam size) best partial hypotheses at each step. Start from <bos>; extend every hypothesis with candidate next tokens; score the extensions by their total (log-)probability; keep only the top k; repeat until the hypotheses end with <eos>; return the most probable complete one.\nAdvantage: it explores more than one path, so it gets closer to the most probable whole sentence than greedy decoding, which can commit early to a token that ruins the rest.\nDisadvantage: much more computation and memory (k hypotheses, and large beams for big models), and it is still not guaranteed optimal. It also tends to reduce diversity. Modern open-ended LLMs use sampling instead.",
