          "type": "written",
          "marks": 10,
          "question": "A decoder has this toy distribution; after any two tokens it outputs </s> with probability 1.\nStep 1: Je 0.45, Il 0.40, </s> 0.15\nAfter \"Je\": suis 0.35, mange 0.35, </s> 0.30\nAfter \"Il\": mange 0.85, est 0.10, </s> 0.05\n(a) Trace greedy decoding and give the output and its probability. (2)\n(b) Trace beam search with $k = 2$, showing the beam after each step, and give the result. (3)\n(c) Use (a) and (b) to explain why $\\prod_t \\arg\\max p(y_t \\mid y_{<t}, x) \\neq \\arg\\max \\prod_t p(y_t \\mid y_{<t}, x)$, and give one advantage and one cost of beam search. (3)\n(d) Explain the information bottleneck in a seq2seq model without attention, and both ways attention relieves it. (2) (10 Marks)",
          "modelAnswer": "(a) Step 1 takes \"Je\" (0.45). Step 2: \"suis\" and \"mange\" tie at 0.35 (either is accepted), then </s>. Output \"Je suis </s>\" (or \"Je mange </s>\"), probability $0.45 \\times 0.35 = 0.1575$.\n\n(b) Step 1: Je (0.45); Il (0.4)\nStep 2: Il mange (0.34); Je suis (0.1575)\nStep 3: Il mange </s> (0.34); Je suis </s> (0.1575)\nResult \"Il mange </s>\" with 0.34, which is also the best sentence overall (0.34).\n\n(c) Greedy maximises each factor separately; the most probable sentence maximises their product. \"Il\" is worse than \"Je\" at step 1 (0.40 < 0.45), but its continuation is far more certain (0.85), so the full sentence wins. Searching every sentence is intractable; beam search keeps $k$ hypotheses so it can recover from a locally worse choice. Advantage: closer to the best whole sentence than greedy. Cost: $k$ times the compute and memory (especially for Transformers), still no guarantee, and it reduces diversity; open-ended LLMs therefore sample instead.\n\n(d) Without attention the whole source is squeezed into the encoder's final state, which initialises the decoder: hard for long sentences, early words fade (recency bias), and the decoder gets the same vector at every step. Attention: (1) encoder side: it no longer has to store every detail in one vector because every encoder state stays available; (2) decoder side: at each step it can fetch the specific source information it needs now, while its recurrent state keeps the overall intent and what it has already said.",
