          "modelAnswer": "Example LIP:\n$$\\min\\ 5x_1 + 3x_2 \\quad \\text{s.t.}\\quad x_1 + x_2 \\ge 2,\\quad x_1, x_2 \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3\\}$$\n(i) Relaxed problem: drop the integer requirement and keep only the bounds, so the variables become real:\n$$\\min\\ 5x_1 + 3x_2 \\quad \\text{s.t.}\\quad x_1 + x_2 \\ge 2,\\quad 0 \\le x_1, x_2 \\le 3$$\nIts feasible set contains every feasible point of the LIP, so its optimal value is a lower bound on the LIP optimum (here the LP optimum is $x = (0, 2)$ with value 6).\n(ii) Upper bound: the objective value of any (integer) feasible solution of the original LIP. For example $(x_1, x_2) = (2, 2)$ is feasible ($2 + 2 = 4 \\ge 2$) and gives $5(2) + 3(2) = 16$, so $16 \\ge \\text{OPT}$. In branch and bound the best feasible solution found so far (the incumbent) is the upper bound and it is lowered whenever a better feasible solution is found.",
