          "modelAnswer": "Let $P$ be a minimisation problem with set of instances $\\mathcal{I}$, and let $A$ be an algorithm that returns a feasible solution $A(I)$ for every instance $I \\in \\mathcal{I}$. $A$ is an $\\alpha$-approximation algorithm ($\\alpha \\ge 1$) if it runs in polynomial time and\n$$A(I) \\le \\alpha \\cdot OPT(I) \\quad \\text{for all } I \\in \\mathcal{I},$$\nwhere $OPT(I)$ is the optimal value of instance $I$. So the solution is never worse than $\\alpha$ times the optimum (e.g. Christofides for the metric STSP has $\\alpha = 3/2$).",
