          "question": "Consider the 7 city symmetric TSP with a feasible tour $x = (1, 3, 2, 6, 7, 4, 5)$ where the 1st city is the starting city. Answer the following questions:\nConstruct two 2-Opt neighbors of $x$. Construct an example of a sub-tour that may occur in the solution process. [2 + 3 Marks ]",
          "modelAnswer": "2-opt move: delete the edges $(c_i, c_{i+1})$ and $(c_j, c_{j+1})$, add $(c_i, c_j)$ and $(c_{i+1}, c_{j+1})$, which reverses positions $i+1, \\dots, j$. Positions (not city labels) of $x$: $c_1 = 1, c_2 = 3, c_3 = 2, c_4 = 6, c_5 = 7, c_6 = 4, c_7 = 5$.\n• $i = 1, j = 3$: remove (1,3) and (2,6), add (1,2) and (3,6), reverse \"3, 2\" → $x^{(1)} = (1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 4, 5)$.\n• $i = 1, j = 4$: remove (1,3) and (6,7), add (1,6) and (3,7), reverse \"3, 2, 6\" → $x^{(2)} = (1, 6, 2, 3, 7, 4, 5)$.\n(Another: $i = 2, j = 4$ gives $(1, 3, 6, 2, 7, 4, 5)$.)\nSub-tour: if the subtour elimination constraints are left out, the degree constraints $\\sum_{e \\in \\delta(i)} x_e = 2$ can be met by several separate cycles instead of one tour, e.g.\n$$1 \\to 3 \\to 2 \\to 1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad 6 \\to 7 \\to 4 \\to 5 \\to 6.$$\nEvery city has degree 2, but the salesman never travels between $\\{1, 2, 3\\}$ and $\\{4, 5, 6, 7\\}$, so it is not a tour. In the STSP a sub-tour needs at least 3 cities (2 cities would need the same undirected edge twice). The constraint $\\sum_{e \\in E(S)} x_e \\le |S| - 1$ for $S = \\{1, 2, 3\\}$ is violated (3 edges > 2), which is how such solutions are cut off.",
