          "type": "written",
          "marks": 6,
          "question": "Construct an example flow network and define an $(s, t)$-cut on it. Let $f(i, j)$ denote the net flow going out of node $i$ to node $j$. Prove that $f(i, j) = -f(j, i)$. [4 + 2 Marks ]",
          "modelAnswer": "Example flow network (capacities on the arcs): $s \\to a$ (4), $s \\to b$ (3), $a \\to b$ (2), $b \\to a$ (1), $a \\to t$ (3), $b \\to t$ (5).\nAn $(s,t)$-cut $(A, B)$ is a partition of the nodes into two disjoint sets with $s \\in A$ and $t \\in B$. On the example: $A = \\{s, a\\}$, $B = \\{b, t\\}$. The cut set is the arcs from $A$ to $B$: $(s,b), (a,b), (a,t)$; removing them leaves no path from $s$ to $t$. Its capacity is the sum of the capacities of the arcs from $A$ to $B$ only: $\\text{cap}(A, B) = 3 + 2 + 3 = 8$ (the arc $b \\to a$ goes from $B$ to $A$ and is not counted).\nProof of skew-symmetry: let $p(i,j) \\ge 0$ be the positive flow on $(i,j)$ and $p(j,i) \\ge 0$ the positive flow on $(j,i)$. The net flow out of $i$ to $j$ is $f(i,j) = p(i,j) - p(j,i)$, and likewise $f(j,i) = p(j,i) - p(i,j)$. Hence\n$$f(j,i) = p(j,i) - p(i,j) = -\\big(p(i,j) - p(j,i)\\big) = -f(i,j).$$",
