          "marks": 2,
          "question": "In the context of Branch and Bound algorithm for a minimization problem answer the following questions: [2 + 3 Marks ]\n(i) What is a relaxed problem?",
          "modelAnswer": "The relaxed problem is the original integer problem with the integrality requirement dropped (e.g. $x_j \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ replaced by $0 \\le x_j \\le 1$, or $x_j \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ by $x_j \\in \\mathbb{R}$), keeping the same objective and all other constraints. Its feasible set contains every feasible point of the original problem, so for a minimisation its optimal value is a lower bound on the original optimum (an upper bound for a maximisation). It is an LP (or a convex QP) and can be solved quickly; B&B solves it at every node.\nExample: for $\\min c^Tx$ s.t. $Ax \\ge b$, $x \\in \\{0,1\\}^n$ the relaxed problem is $\\min c^Tx$ s.t. $Ax \\ge b$, $0 \\le x \\le 1$.",
