          "concept": "Regression types; bipartite graphs",
          "type": "written",
          "marks": 6,
          "question": "Differentiate between (i) linear regression and non-linear regression, (ii) Bipartite and complete bipartite graph. You may use example graphs in (ii). [3 + 3 Marks ]",
          "modelAnswer": "(i) Linear regression: the model is linear in its unknown parameters, $\\hat{f}(x) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 g_1(x) + \\dots + \\beta_p g_p(x)$, so the data give a linear system $A\\beta = y$ (the basis functions $g_k$ themselves may be non-linear, e.g. polynomials). The least-squares parameters come in closed form from the normal equation $A^TA\\beta = A^Ty$.\nNon-linear regression: the model depends non-linearly on the parameters, $y = f(x, \\theta) + \\epsilon$ (e.g. $\\theta_1 e^{\\theta_2 x}$). There is no closed form; the parameters are found iteratively with non-linear optimisation (e.g. conjugate gradient or Newton-type methods), which may stop at a local minimum.\n(ii) Bipartite graph: the vertices split into two disjoint sets $U$ and $W$ so that every edge joins a vertex of $U$ to a vertex of $W$ (no edge inside $U$ or inside $W$). Not every $U$–$W$ pair has to be joined. Example: $U = \\{a, b, c\\}$, $W = \\{d, e\\}$, edges $a$–$d$, $b$–$d$, $c$–$e$.\nComplete bipartite graph $K_{m,n}$: a bipartite graph in which every vertex of $U$ ($|U| = m$) is joined to every vertex of $W$ ($|W| = n$), so it has exactly $m \\cdot n$ edges. Example: $K_{3,3}$, three workers each joined to all three jobs (the linear assignment graph).",
