          "concept": "Maximum clique and planted clique",
          "type": "written",
          "marks": 5,
          "question": "Define the maximum clique with an example graph. Describe one way of creating a planted clique of size $k$. [2 + 3 Marks ]",
          "modelAnswer": "A clique of a graph $G = (V, E)$ is a subset $C \\subseteq V$ in which every pair of vertices is joined by an edge (the vertices induce a complete subgraph). The maximum clique is a clique with the largest possible number of vertices; its size is the clique number $\\omega(G)$.\nExample: $V = \\{1, \\dots, 6\\}$, edges 1–2, 1–3, 1–4, 2–3, 2–4, 3–4, 3–5, 4–5, 5–6, 6–1. $\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ is a clique (all 6 pairs are edges) and there is no clique of 5 vertices, so the maximum clique is $\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ with size 4. ($\\{3, 4, 5\\}$ is a clique but not maximum.)\nPlanted clique of size $k$:\n1. Generate a random graph $G(n, \\tfrac{1}{2})$: for every pair of the $n$ vertices, add the edge independently with probability $\\tfrac12$.\n2. Choose $k$ of the $n$ vertices uniformly at random.\n3. Add every missing edge between the chosen vertices, so they form a clique of size $k$ (the \"planted\" clique).\nThe task is then to find the hidden clique. Since a random $G(n, \\tfrac12)$ only has cliques of about $2\\log_2 n$ vertices, a planted clique with $k$ clearly larger than that is the unique maximum clique and can be used as a test instance with a known answer.",
